Fixes GURI-2114 (Won cars drop as tradable Steam inventory items), with GURI-2115 (Steam seam: optional GodotSteam GDExtension behind a SteamService), GURI-2116 (Steam car item definitions generated from the game's car rules), GURI-2117 (A Steam item rebuilds the same car on every client), GURI-2118 (Winning a car in a run asks Steam for a car drop), GURI-2119 (Garage mirrors the player's Steam cars, with an offline cache) and GURI-2120 (Steamworks runbook: car items, drop limits and partner testing).

## Why

Fran wants cars won in runs to be Steam items players can trade. Valve's docs (cited in GURI-2114) decide the shape:
- A shipped client cannot grant a chosen item.
- Dynamic properties are wiped on trade.
- Only tags, which Steam rolls, survive a trade.

Fran's calls:
- Steam rolls the car: a run win triggers a playtime drop.
- Tradable, not marketable.
- Steam is optional.

## What Changed

- **`SteamCars`** (`scripts/steam/steam_cars.gd`) owns ids, names, weights and drop limits. It generates:
  - the Steamworks item definitions: one tradable, unmarketable item per generator car type; a hidden playtime generator; three tag generators for the zero-sum stat pairs (pace speed/tough, bite grip/drift, punch accel/boost, 9 buckets each);
  - the English tag strings Steam requires.

  An item (definition + tags) decodes into the same car on every client: the roll goes through `RunState.roll_from_pairs`, now shared with run rolls, and the look comes from the type and buckets.
- **`SteamService`** wraps GodotSteam:
  - `refresh()` mirrors the inventory;
  - `trigger_car_drop()` asks for a drop;
  - it is a no-op without the extension, without the client, or in tests.
- **App:**
  - a duel or lockup car win asks for a drop;
  - drops queue and show as a banner over the next run screen;
  - the last inventory read is cached in the save (`steam_cars`), so Quick Race lists Steam cars offline;
  - opening the garage re-reads the inventory.

  No save version bump: the key is a cache that older builds drop harmlessly.
- **Garage:** cards show a STEAM tape and "IN YOUR STEAM INVENTORY". Origin copy goes through one `RUN_UI.origin_line`.
- **Tools:**
  - `tools/fetch_godotsteam.sh` installs GodotSteam 4.22.1, sha256-pinned and not committed;
  - `tools/export_steam_itemdefs.gd` writes the upload file and the tag strings.
- **Docs:** the owner's Steamworks steps are in `docs/steam-release.md`.

## Verification

- New `steam_cars_test` covers:
  - the definitions (types, flags, generator, tag generators, every rollable value decodes, every tag has an English string);
  - the decode (deterministic, zero-sum, inside the envelope, malformed items rejected);
  - the service with a fake Steam (drop, nothing due, failed result, handles freed, no Steam);
  - the App (duel and lockup ask for a drop, a drop during a race waits for the next run screen, a new night clears queued drops, Quick Race and the save get the car, a traded-away car leaves).
- Mutations that each make it fail:
  - no drop on a duel;
  - no cache save;
  - a decode that ignores a pair;
  - no queue clear on a new night.
- Local, isolated user data, all PASS with markers:
  - `steam_cars`, `garage`, `save_store`, `championship_catalog`
  - `run_session`, `run_offer`, `run_board`, `run_rewards`, `run_node_outcomes`
  - `app_shell_focus`, `discovery_controller_flow`, `route_board_regression`, `track_length_selection`
  - `championship_flow`, `controller_flow`, `menu_feedback`
- Run with and without the addon installed: `steam_cars`, `run_board` and `app_shell_focus` pass both ways.
- A real boot with GodotSteam and no Steam client logs "did not locate a running instance of Steam" and plays on.
- Garage capture with Steam cars reviewed.
- `validate_release_config` PASS.
- A fresh-eyes review checked the generated JSON field by field against Valve's schema and itemtags docs, and the GodotSteam 4.22.1 source. Its findings are fixed in the second commit:
  - tag localization;
  - icons;
  - same-name cars;
  - the drop queue;
  - save-error clobbering;
  - refresh on garage open;
  - docs wording.

## Not verified here (needs the owner)

A real drop, and a trade between two accounts. Both need the app id, the uploaded definitions and tag strings, and partner test accounts. See the runbook.

Adds [appstore-release](https://github.com/beydemirfurkan/appstore-release) under Developer Tools, next to the other App Store Connect servers.

It is a local MCP server, zero-dependency Node and MIT-licensed, with 13 outcome-shaped tools. It takes an iOS app to App Store review:
- a readiness report that says what blocks submission and who can fix it;
- an idempotent listing pipeline;
- an offline preflight of the app project;
- a submit that refuses unless the report says ready.

Every write needs `confirm: true`, and credentials come only from environment variables. It is also listed in the MCP Registry as `io.github.beydemirfurkan/appstore-release`.

